    "body": "Cobalt in the DRC is often found alongside copper and malachite. (Image by Fairphone, Flickr ).\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 23, 2026\nThe Democratic Republic of Congo will suspend all cobalt exports from Nov. 30 as prices slide despite restrictions on shipments from the world’s top producer of the battery metal, Reuters and Bloomberg reported Monday, citing the country’s strategic minerals regulator.\nThe ban covers cobalt from industrial and artisanal mines and has no fixed end date. The regulator, known by its French acronym Arecoms, plans to review the measure after four months and use the suspension to revise how it allocates export quotas. It cited rising inventories and weaker consumption.\nCongo supplied about three-quarters of the world’s mined cobalt before it first halted shipments in February 2025. Prices initially rose, but cobalt hydroxide, the main product Congo ships, lost nearly half its value from an April peak, according to Fastmarkets. The Cobalt Institute’s latest quarterly report put the cobalt hydroxide price in Asia at $14.50 (C$20.85) per lb. of contained cobalt on Oct. 14, down 43% since July 1, citing Benchmark Mineral Intelligence data.\nThe quotas already left a wide gap between what miners produced and what they could sell. China’s CMOC Group produced 99,694 tonnes of cobalt in the first nine months of 2026 and sold just 12,661 tonnes, leaving a difference of 87,033 tonnes between production and sales in that period. Output rose 13% from a year earlier while sales fell 75%.\nSales impact\nGlencore (LSE: GLEN) took a different approach. Its cobalt output fell more than half to 13,700 tonnes over the same period. As it focused on copper, Glencore left more of the cobalt it recovered from mixed ore in solution, deferring processing into saleable hydroxide, the company said in its quarterly production report.\nKinshasa replaced its first export ban with quotas in October 2025, setting an export ceiling of 96,600 tonnes for 2026. Of that, 87,000 tonnes were available to producers and 9,600 tonnes were set aside for projects the regulator deemed strategically important.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: cobalt\nREGION: Africa\nCOMPANY: CMOC Glencore\nRepublish this article",
